**Corollary 8.2 (finite certificates have the original memory consumer).**
If $V_{\rm swap}(d,L,\mu,C_0,\pi)$ accepts, then every rational
$\widehat C>0$ satisfying

$$
\widehat C^{L+1}\ge4^{m_d}C_0^{L+1}
\tag{OOA.34}
$$

is an unrestricted all-template cap. In particular, against every
original competitor and for every $D\ge d$,

$$
\beta_d(\mu)\le\widehat C,\qquad
M_1(D)\ge\left\lceil\widehat C^{-2^{D-d}}\right\rceil,
\qquad \lambda\ge-2^{-d}\log_2\widehat C.
\tag{OOA.35}
$$

Proof. Theorem 6.3 gives $\beta_{d,L}(\mu)\le C_0$. The credited
[SIR, Theorem 4.1][SIR] preserves actual owner sections and every
once-owned weight, giving
$\beta_d(\mu)\le4^{m_d/(L+1)}\beta_{d,L}(\mu)$.
Equation (OOA.34) bounds this factor by $\widehat C$.
[NPC, Theorem 7.1][NPC], or the uniform version of Theorem 8.1,
then supplies the memory and rate implications. Both directions of
the RRN correspondence preserve original block count; the SIR factor
is explicitly paid in this analytical transfer. These are uses of
credited transport, with the new finite rule as input. $\square$

